{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792713451,"nonce":"de/aJnrwpryCXEM0nwXlfozKz9M=","title":"Reuse native application menus across tab changes","message":"The new native menu integration rebuilds NSApp.mainMenu on every active-tab/dirty/focus update. Matching native AOT 65-tab workload: before multiwindow 299.4 MiB open / 200.5 MiB closed idle; after multiwindow 395.0 MiB open / 292.2 MiB closed idle. Managed occupied blocks differ only about 7 MiB. CoreAnimation regions grow from 20 to 3860, with about 31 MiB backing storage, plus native malloc growth. The live user process with 38 saved tabs was 508.0 MiB (peak 518.0), with 3705 CoreAnimation regions.\n\nUpdate the existing NSMenu/NSMenuItem hierarchy in place, including added/removed submenu contents, shortcuts, enabled states, command ids, and per-window focus activation. Use a local autorelease pool at the native install boundary. Native regression covers stable identities over 200 refreshes, command replacement/dispatch, shortcut removal, enabled changes and submenu insertion/removal. The same standalone allocation probe falls from 6.9 MiB of pending allocations over 200 refreshes to about 64 KiB. Full windowed comparison and validation will be recorded after rebuilding the native library.","files":null}]}